  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to optimize a parameterized family of sequences using Bayesian optimization with Gaussian Process (GP) surrogate models. This differs fundamentally from the previous LP-based approach by:\n\n1. Directly optimizing the sequence using a parameterized basis representation (instead of projecting arbitrary sequences)\n2. Leveraging the non-convex structure of the problem through Bayesian optimization\n3. Using domain-specific constraints and objectives without needing to solve multiple LP phases\n\nThis method is more efficient for expensive black-box optimization like this non-convex AC1 minimization problem. Unlike previous strategies that used sequences as fixed-length vectors, here we represent sequences with fewer parameters, allowing faster exploration while maintaining flexibility. The gradient information from the evaluators optimization method isn't needed because we directly learn the objective function.\n\nMathematical specification\n1. Representation: \n   - Sequence of length n=32 (default, but lengths can vary)\n   - Defined by 4 coefficients (w0, w1, w2, w3) for a weighted sum of basis functions\n   - Basis functions: polynomial (degree 0-5), harmonic (frequencies 0-4), gaussian (widths 0.1-0.4)\n\n2. Variables: \n   - 4 real parameters w for each basis element\n   - Coefficient bounds: [0, 1000]\n   - Element-wise clipping enforced\n\n3. Exact objective:\n   - Minimize score(a) = 2*n*max(convolve(a,a))/sum(a)**2\n   - Constraints: sum(a)>=0.01 after clipping\n\n4. Basis functions:\n   - polynomials: (x-a)^k * e^{-k|3a-x|} to ensure positiveness near maxima\n   - harmonics: sin(2\u03c0freq*x)^2 + 0.1*cos(4\u03c0freq*x) with clipping\n   - gaussians: e^{-0.5( (x-center)/\u03c3 )^2} centered at points 0, n/5, n/2, 3n/5, n\n\n   Each basis shape function \u03b2_k(x) is defined, and a_j = \u2211_k w_k \u03b2_k(j/n)\n\n5. Sampling strategy:\n   - Sample coefficients uniformly in [min_co,max_co] with:\n        min_co = [0.1,0.1,0.1,0.1]\n        max_co = [3.0,3.0,3.0,3.0]\n   - Use quasi-Monte Carlo for sampling new points\n\n6. Acquisition function:\n   - Expected improvement (EI)\n   - Incorporates dimension-adaptive weighting for efficient exploration\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Initialization:\n   - Read evaluator and clip implementation\n   - Set parameters: \n        n_sample_points = 200\n        n_dim = 4\n        min_co = [0.5,0.1,0.1,0.1]   # scaled minima\n        max_co = [3.0,3.0,3.0,3.0]   # maximum values\n        seed42 = provided integer\n\n2. Bayesian Optimization Loop:\n   - Acquire current time\n   - Initialize with random uniform points in n_sample_points\n\n   For each iteration:\n     a) Evaluate all sampled points using evaluator()\n        - Clean up sequences and clip\n        - Skip points that fail evaluator\n     b) Build GP surrogate from feasible points\n        - Use scikit-learn Gaussian Process with Mat\u00e9rn kernel\n        - Compute derivative and uncertainty estimates\n     c) Compute expected improvement (EI) across entire space\n        - Balance exploration vs exploitation using dimension-adaptive inflation\n     d) Select best expansion point (EI_with_adapt)\n     e) Calculate new point with constrained random walk\n     f) Evaluate new point and update GP database\n\n   - Perform up to 1000 evaluations\n   - Add one-dimensional variations around best point\n\n3. Candidate acceptance:\n   - Track best candidate found by exact evaluator()\n   - For each new candidate, verify:\n        - Sequence length preserved\n        - Sum >= 0.01 after clipping\n        - Coefficients in [0, 1000]\n   - Keep track of best AC1 score and sequence\n\n4. Termination:\n   - Time budget: 1300 seconds\n   - Stop when time running out or score improvement < 0.0001\n   - Return sequence with best score found\n\nSearch choices and useful variations\n1. Default configuration: \n   - Basis of 4 parameters with polynomial/harmonic/gaussian family\n   - Parameter range: [0.5,3] for weights\n   - 200 sample points per optimization cycle\n\n2. Use variants for:\n   Basis_variation: Change basis functions (P=0.7)\n   Length_variation: Sample 3 different sequence lengths (P=0.3)\n   Parameter_variation: Use different parameter spaces (P=0.2)\n\n3. Abort rules:\n   Score_dominated: If score > 1.52, abort in favor of new structure\n   Time_critical: Monitor elapsed time and reduce sampling density\n\nComputebudget and robustness\n1. Time estimation:\n   - Initial: Create basis vectors O(n) = 32 operations\n   - Convolution: O(n log n) using FFT\n   - GP fitting: O(m n_dim) = 200 * 4 ~ 800 operations\n\n2. Memory: ~500 points * n_dim parameters ~ 2,000 real numbers\n\n3. Deadline policy:\n   - Check budget every 200 evaluations\n   - Abort when budget < 500 seconds remaining\n   - Fallback: Linear interpolation from best candidate\n\n4. Fallback mechanism:\n   - Preserve base coefficients and apply random jitter\n   - Clip all values to [0,1000]\n   - Sum normalized to 1.0\n\nValidation and return contract\n1. Feasibility checks:\n   - verify is_list and is_number array\n   - apply_clipping(sequence)\n   - refund to parent sequence if needed\n\n2. Scoring protocol:\n   - Call evaluator(sequence) for each candidate\n   - Track best_valid_score across runs\n   - Requires sequence length 32, not length-episodic modifications\n\n3. Return format:\n   - List of exactly 32 floating point values\n   - Values in [0,1000]\n   - Evaluates to score <= 1.509179\n\nLow-level PC implementation details:\n   def safe_convolve(a):\n        # safely compute full linear convolution\n   def score_function(params):\n        # map parameters to sequence then evaluate\n   def gaussian_process(x, y):\n        # basic GP with matern kernel",
